I found the research you were looking for. The paper "Time Travel in LLMs: Tracing Data Contamination in Large Language Models" by Golchin and Surdeanu was published in Proceedings of the Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) in 2024.

The paper addresses data contamination, i.e., the presence of test data from downstream tasks in the training data of large language models (LLMs), which is a potential major issue in measuring LLMs' real effectiveness on other tasks.

Here is the BibTeX citation for this paper:

@inproceedings{golchin2024time, title={Time Travel in {LLM}s: Tracing Data Contamination in Large Language Models}, author={Shahriar Golchin and Mihai Surdeanu}, booktitle={Proceedings of the Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)}, year={2024}, url={https://openreview.net/forum?id=2Rwq6c3tvr} }

The paper's method employs "guided instruction" consisting of the dataset name, partition type, and the random-length initial segment of a reference instance to estimate contamination of individual instances.

